---
import Journey from '../../layouts/Journey.astro';
import Step from '../../components/journey/Step.astro';
import More from '../../components/journey/More.astro';
import Next from '../../components/journey/Next.astro';
import Fact from '../../components/data/Fact.astro';
import Verified from '../../components/data/Verified.astro';
import CopyPrompt from '../../components/site/CopyPrompt.astro';
import { models } from '../../data/models';
import { t3code } from '../../data/t3code';
import { url } from '../../lib/nav';

// Who builds and who reviews. Swap the two keys and the chapter swaps the names; nothing else in
// the script depends on which model is which.
const builder = models['claude-opus-5-5'];
const reviewer = models['gpt-6-2-sol'];
const short = (name: string) => name.replace(/^Claude /, '');
const data = {
  builder: short(builder.name), reviewer: short(reviewer.name),
  builderLab: builder.lab, reviewerLab: reviewer.lab,
  handoffCap: t3code.handoffCap.value,
};
const ids = [
  'harnesses.claudeCode.codeReview', 'harnesses.claudeCode.reviewerAlwaysFinds', 'plans.claude.pro.claudeCode', 'plans.chatgpt.limits.codexOnEveryPlan',
  't3code.handoff', 't3code.handoffCap', 'harnesses.codex.sandbox',
];
---
<Journey chapter="review" first="self" data={data} title="A second model" description="One model builds, a different model reviews with a fresh window, and they loop until the real bugs are gone.">
  <Step id="self" title="Asked to check itself, it trusts its own work">
    <p>When {data.builder} reviews its own code, it reads back through the same context window it just built in, carrying the same assumptions. Out of three planted bugs in this example, it catches one—the other two slip past <a href={url('journey/check/')}>the check from step 5</a> and reach you.</p>
  </Step>
  <Step id="second" title="A different model starts with fresh eyes">
    <p>Send the diff across to {data.reviewer} instead, in an empty window holding only your brief and the change. Without the builder’s assumptions, it catches the flaws and sends them back to be fixed until a round comes back clean.</p>
    <More title="Where this comes from">
      <p><Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.codeReview" show="claim" /></p>
    </More>
  </Step>
  <Step id="attack" title="Tell it to try to break it">
    <p>A polite “Review this” usually earns a rubber stamp and a minor naming suggestion. Tell {data.reviewer} to hunt for bugs and try to break the code, and the real flaws surface.</p>
    <div class="pick">
      <button type="button" data-do="brief" data-val="polite" data-default aria-pressed="true">“Review this.”</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="brief" data-val="attack" aria-pressed="false">“Find what is wrong. Try to break it.”</button>
    </div>
    <More title="A prompt for this">
      <CopyPrompt title="Ask a second model for an adversarial review" compact>{`You are reviewing a change you did not write. You get the task and the diff below, and nothing else: do not ask for the author's explanation.

Find the three most likely ways this change is wrong: a case it mishandles, someone it lets do too much, data it breaks. For each, give a way to check it: a command, a test to write, or steps to follow in the app. Skip style and naming. If you find fewer than three real problems, say so.

Task: <paste the task you gave the first model>

Diff: <paste the output of git diff>`}</CopyPrompt>
    </More>
  </Step>
  <Step id="rounds" title="Set a stop rule">
    <p>Each round drains both providers’ allowances. Without a stop rule, a reviewer will always find something to complain about—once the real bugs are gone, it keeps sending back style nitpicks forever.</p>
    <div class="pick">
      <button type="button" data-do="rule" data-val="none" data-default aria-pressed="true">No limit</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="rule" data-val="rule" aria-pressed="false">Only what breaks, three rounds at most</button>
    </div>
    <More title="Where this comes from">
      <p><Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.reviewerAlwaysFinds" show="claim" /></p>
    </More>
  </Step>
  <Step id="how" title="How to run the loop">
    <p>You can paste findings between two threads by hand, hand off one thread to another model, or let <a href={url('journey/lead/')}>the lead from step 8</a> run the back-and-forth and bring you one summary. Either model can build or review.</p>
    <div class="pick">
      <button type="button" data-do="how" data-val="hand" data-default aria-pressed="true">By hand</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="how" data-val="handoff" aria-pressed="false">One thread, handed off</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="how" data-val="lead" aria-pressed="false">A lead runs the loop</button>
    </div>
    <More title="In T3 Code and Codex">
      <p><Fact id="t3code.handoff" show="claim" /> The selection holds <Fact id="t3code.handoffCap" /> by default. <Fact id="harnesses.codex.sandbox" show="claim" /></p>
    </More>
    <Next from="review" more={[{ href: 'explain/verification/', text: 'Checking the work' }, { href: 'models/', text: 'The models' }]} />
  </Step>
  <Fragment slot="foot">
    <p>The example feature, its three bugs, the review comments, and the plan drain rates are illustrative. Which model builds and which reviews can be swapped—the point is that they are different. <Fact id="plans.claude.pro.claudeCode" show="claim" /> <Fact id="plans.chatgpt.limits.codexOnEveryPlan" show="claim" /></p>
    <Verified ids={ids} />
  </Fragment>
</Journey>
